# Copy to .env on the NAS and chmod 600. .env is gitignored at the repo root.
#
#   cp .env.example .env && chmod 600 .env
#
# Unlike the other stacks, docker-compose.yml does NOT pass this whole file to
# every container. Each service is handed only the variables it names, so the
# SMTP password below is in the reporter's environment and no other. A variable
# added here does nothing until a service in docker-compose.yml names it.
#
# WHAT A LEAKED COPY OF THIS FILE COSTS: the mail account behind SMTP_PASSWORD
# (a Gmail app password reads the whole mailbox -- use a dedicated sending
# account if that matters), and a Beszel login that can read graphs. Not the
# Beszel admin: no admin password is kept here at all (README.md#setup).

# Published by .github/workflows/build-monitoring.yml. This is the weekly
# reporter only; Beszel and the socket proxy are pinned by digest in
# docker-compose.yml and do not read this.
IMAGE=ghcr.io/ky1ejs/homelab/monitoring:latest

# Enables provenance verification in `homelab deploy`, as for the other stacks.
GITHUB_OWNER=ky1ejs

# Must match every other stack. See obsidian-vault/DECISIONS.md#identity-1002-not-1000.
APP_UID=1002
APP_GID=100

# Where the hub's database, the agent's identity, the shared socket and the
# disk-usage probe live. Four subdirectories, created by `homelab setup monitoring`:
# hub/, agent/, socket/, fs-probe/ -- all owned by APP_UID:APP_GID.
#
# On the CE_ volume, like everything else here, so it stays inside the
# encrypted set. The data is metrics, not notes, but the hub's database also
# holds the admin password hash, the hub's SSH key, and the SMTP password you
# type into its settings page. Deliberately outside obsidian/, which
# obsidian-vault/ARCHITECTURE.md#volumes enumerates for the other stack.
#
# fs-probe/ must be on the DATA volume, because its free space is what the
# "disk data" graph and the weekly report's projection measure.
MON_DATA_HOST_PATH=/share/CE_CACHEDEV4_DATA/monitoring

# --- The dashboard ---------------------------------------------------------

# The hub binds 127.0.0.1:${MON_PORT}. `tailscale serve` on the host puts it at
# MON_APP_URL with a real certificate:
#
#   tailscale serve --bg --https 8443 http://127.0.0.1:8091
#
# 8091 because the dashboard has 8088. A different HTTPS port (8443) rather
# than a path under the dashboard's 443 because a different port is a different
# ORIGIN, and the browser stops a page's script reading or scripting pages on
# another origin. Under /monitoring/ on 443, Beszel's third-party JavaScript
# would share an origin with the deploy buttons and could send their required
# X-Homelab-Action header itself. (Cookies are NOT separated by port, so do not
# lean on this for one; the dashboard sets none.) Beszel itself would cope with
# a path (APP_URL takes one); that is not the reason.
MON_PORT=8091

# The URL Serve answers on. Used for links in Beszel's alert emails and in the
# weekly report. No trailing slash.
MON_APP_URL=https://kyles-nas.tail3df177.ts.net:8443

# The compose network, pinned because the hub trusts the identity header only
# from its gateway (TRUSTED_PROXY_IPS in docker-compose.yml) -- the address
# connections through the loopback publish arrive from. Change both together,
# only if the subnet collides with an existing Docker network on the host.
# MON_GATEWAY must be the first address in MON_SUBNET.
MON_SUBNET=172.31.91.0/28
MON_GATEWAY=172.31.91.1

# No admin email or password here, deliberately. The first account is created on
# the hub's first-run page (`homelab setup monitoring` does it), which makes it an admin and the
# /_/ superuser at once and leaves no password in any container's environment.

# The hub's public key, which the agent uses to decide who may read the socket.
# Leave blank: `homelab setup monitoring` reads it from the hub and writes it
# here, and writes the new one if the hub is ever rebuilt. The agent restarts
# in a loop until this is set, which is expected before setup and nothing else.
MON_HUB_KEY=

# Optional dead-man's switch. The hub pings here every 60 seconds, as a bare
# HEAD request (docker-compose.yml sets HEARTBEAT_METHOD), so the third party
# learns that the hub is up and nothing else. Point it at a Healthchecks.io
# check (free tier; period 5 min, grace 10 min) and Healthchecks emails you
# when the pings stop -- which happens when the hub dies, the NAS loses power,
# or the internet connection drops. It is the only alert in this
# stack that can fire when the NAS is off, because it is the only one that is
# not on the NAS. Blank disables it.
MON_HEARTBEAT_URL=

# --- The weekly report -----------------------------------------------------

# The reporter's own Beszel account, role `readonly`. NOT your account: the
# reporter refuses to run as anything but readonly, so this password is worth a
# view of the graphs. Leave both blank: `homelab setup monitoring` creates the
# account with a generated password and writes them here.
REPORT_BESZEL_EMAIL=
REPORT_BESZEL_PASSWORD=

# "<weekday> HH:MM" in the NAS's local time. Monday morning, so the week it
# covers is a whole working week plus the weekend before it.
REPORT_SCHEDULE=Mon 08:00

# Who it goes to, and who it says it is from. REPORT_TO takes a
# comma-separated list. With Gmail, REPORT_FROM must be the Gmail account (or
# an alias verified on it), or Gmail rewrites it.
REPORT_FROM=
REPORT_TO=

# SMTP submission. Port 587 needs STARTTLS and 465 is implicit TLS; the
# reporter refuses to send over any connection that is not encrypted.
#
# For Gmail: smtp.gmail.com, 587, your address as the username, and an APP
# PASSWORD as the password (Google Account > Security > 2-Step Verification >
# App passwords). Your real password will not work, and should not be here.
# The hub's alert emails need SMTP too, typed into its /_/ mail settings: give
# it a SECOND app password, typed when `homelab setup monitoring` asks, so each
# can be revoked alone.
SMTP_HOST=smtp.gmail.com
SMTP_PORT=587
SMTP_USERNAME=
SMTP_PASSWORD=

# Optional. Pinged after each report is built AND sent. Point it at a second
# Healthchecks.io check with a period of 7 days and a grace of 1 day, and a
# Monday with no email becomes a Tuesday alert instead of a silence nobody
# notices. Blank disables it.
REPORT_PING_URL=

# --- Memory ----------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Small, and deliberately set: this host already overcommits (the other
# stacks' limits add up to more than its free RAM -- README.md#the-host), and
# monitoring is the wrong thing to be the cause of that getting worse. On an
# idle test run the hub used 13 MB, the agent 5 MB and the proxy 3 MB; the
# hub's limit leaves room for a month of history and page loads on top.
BESZEL_MEM_LIMIT=256m
BESZEL_AGENT_MEM_LIMIT=128m
DOCKER_PROXY_MEM_LIMIT=32m
REPORT_MEM_LIMIT=64m
